            {
              title: {
                1: 'The succah case and Bill 21',
                2: 'The succah on the balcony · Two Canadian approaches to religious symbols',
                3: 'The succah on the balcony · Two Canadian approaches to religious symbols (B3.2)'
              },
              visual: '🏘️',
              body: {
                1: '**Amselem (2004).** Orthodox Jewish residents of a Montreal condo built small succahs on their balconies for Sukkot. The bylaws banned balcony structures. The Supreme Court ruled 5–4 for the residents. Courts only check if a belief is **sincere** — not whether religious leaders require it.\n\n**Two approaches to religious symbols**\n- **Ontario and most of Canada:** public employees, including teachers and police, may wear religious dress.\n- **Quebec: Bill 21 (2019)** bans public employees in positions of authority — judges, police, teachers — from wearing religious symbols at work. Supporters want a visibly secular state. Critics say it falls hardest on Muslim women and Sikh men.',
                2: '**Syndicat Northcrest v. Amselem (2004).** Moïse Amselem and other Orthodox Jewish residents of a Montreal condominium built small succahs on their balconies for the nine days of Sukkot. The condo\'s bylaws banned structures on balconies, and the board offered a shared succah in the garden instead. The residents said their personal religious belief required each household to have its own. In 2004 the Supreme Court of Canada ruled 5–4 for the residents. The majority held that freedom of religion protects a sincerely held belief even if religious authorities do not require the practice, that the court\'s job is only to check sincerity, and that a nine-day temporary structure caused the condo very little harm.\n\n| Ontario and most of Canada | Quebec: Bill 21 (2019) |\n| Public employees, including teachers and police, may wear religious dress | Bans public employees in positions of authority — judges, police, teachers — from wearing religious symbols at work |\n| Since 1990 the RCMP has allowed turbans in uniform | Supporters: the state should be neutral and visibly secular (laïcité); surveys showed majority support in Quebec |\n| The Code requires accommodation of creed in employment and services | Critics: it falls hardest on Muslim women in hijab and Sikh men in turbans and violates freedom of religion |\n| Debate centres on individual cases, not blanket rules | Passed using the Charter\'s notwithstanding clause; challenged in court; the Supreme Court of Canada heard the case in March 2026 |',
                3: '**Syndicat Northcrest v. Amselem (2004).** Moïse Amselem and other Orthodox Jewish residents of a Montreal condominium built small succahs on their balconies for the nine days of Sukkot. The condo\'s bylaws banned structures on balconies, and the board offered a shared succah in the garden instead. The residents said their personal religious belief required each household to have its own. In 2004 the Supreme Court of Canada ruled 5–4 for the residents. The majority held that freedom of religion protects a sincerely held belief even if religious authorities do not require the practice, that the court\'s job is only to check sincerity, and that a nine-day temporary structure caused the condo very little harm.\n\n**Think about it:** What did the Court say about who decides what a religion "requires"? Four judges disagreed — the dissent worried about the rights of other owners and about courts accepting purely personal interpretations without evidence. (2004 SCC 47; judgment June 30, 2004. This is the origin of the "sincerity of belief" test used in Multani and in creed cases under the Ontario Code.)\n\n| Ontario and most of Canada | Quebec: Bill 21 (2019) |\n| Public employees, including teachers and police, may wear religious dress | Bans public employees in positions of authority — judges, police, teachers — from wearing religious symbols at work |\n| Since 1990 the RCMP has allowed turbans in uniform | Supporters: the state should be neutral and visibly secular (laïcité); surveys showed majority support in Quebec |\n| The Code requires accommodation of creed in employment and services | Critics: it falls hardest on Muslim women in hijab and Sikh men in turbans and violates freedom of religion |\n| Debate centres on individual cases, not blanket rules | Passed using the Charter\'s notwithstanding clause; challenged in court; the Supreme Court of Canada heard the case in March 2026 |\n\n**From the speaker notes:** both sides are sincere. Bill 21 (An Act respecting the laicity of the State) passed June 16, 2019; the Quebec Court of Appeal upheld it in 2024; the Supreme Court heard the appeals in March 2026 — check the outcome. The RCMP turban decision came in March 1990 after Baltej Singh Dhillon applied to join the force.',
                4: '**Syndicat Northcrest v. Amselem (2004).** Moïse Amselem and other Orthodox Jewish residents of a Montreal condominium built small succahs on their balconies for the nine days of Sukkot. The condo\'s bylaws banned structures on balconies, and the board offered a shared succah in the garden instead. The residents said their personal religious belief required each household to have its own. In 2004 the Supreme Court of Canada ruled 5–4 for the residents. The majority held that freedom of religion protects a sincerely held belief even if religious authorities do not require the practice, that the court\'s job is only to check sincerity, and that a nine-day temporary structure caused the condo very little harm.\n\n**Think about it:** What did the Court say about who decides what a religion "requires"? Four judges disagreed — the dissent worried about the rights of other owners and about courts accepting purely personal interpretations without evidence. (2004 SCC 47; judgment June 30, 2004. This is the origin of the "sincerity of belief" test used in Multani and in creed cases under the Ontario Code.)\n\n| Ontario and most of Canada | Quebec: Bill 21 (2019) |\n| Public employees, including teachers and police, may wear religious dress | Bans public employees in positions of authority — judges, police, teachers — from wearing religious symbols at work |\n| Since 1990 the RCMP has allowed turbans in uniform | Supporters: the state should be neutral and visibly secular (laïcité); surveys showed majority support in Quebec |\n| The Code requires accommodation of creed in employment and services | Critics: it falls hardest on Muslim women in hijab and Sikh men in turbans and violates freedom of religion |\n| Debate centres on individual cases, not blanket rules | Passed using the Charter\'s notwithstanding clause; challenged in court; the Supreme Court of Canada heard the case in March 2026 |\n\n**From the speaker notes:** both sides are sincere. Bill 21 (An Act respecting the laicity of the State) passed June 16, 2019; the Quebec Court of Appeal upheld it in 2024; the Supreme Court heard the appeals in March 2026 — check the outcome. The RCMP turban decision came in March 1990 after Baltej Singh Dhillon applied to join the force.\n\n**Go further**\n\nAmselem and Bill 21 pull in opposite directions, and seeing why is the point of placing them together. Amselem widens protection: a sincere personal belief counts even when no authority requires it, and the court weighs harm to others (nine days, little harm) rather than theology. Bill 21 narrows visible belief for a category of workers in the name of state neutrality, and its supporters and critics both claim to be protecting freedom — of the public from religion, or of the individual to practise. The 5–4 split in Amselem and the use of the notwithstanding clause for Bill 21 are both signs that the line between accommodation and neutrality is still being drawn.\n\n**Think like a scholar**\n- Compare the Amselem dissent\'s worry (personal interpretations without evidence) with Bill 21\'s goal (a visibly neutral state). Are they the same objection in different clothes?\n- Predict how the RCMP\'s 1990 turban decision would be treated under Bill 21\'s logic, and what that comparison reveals.'
              },
              mod: {
                k2: 'Some Jewish families built small huts on balconies. It was for a holiday. The building said no. The judges said yes. Judges ask if a belief is honest.',
                g35: 'In 2004, in the Amselem case, Jewish residents of a Montreal condo built small **succahs**, temporary huts for the festival of Sukkot, on their balconies. The building banned balcony structures. The Supreme Court ruled 5–4 for the residents: courts only check if a belief is sincere, not whether leaders require it. In Ontario, teachers and police may wear religious dress. Quebec\'s Bill 21 (2019) bans some public workers from wearing religious symbols. Supporters want a neutral state; critics say it hurts Muslim women and Sikh men most.',
                g68: '**Amselem (2004).** Orthodox Jewish residents of a Montreal condo built small succahs on their balconies for Sukkot. The bylaws banned balcony structures. The Supreme Court ruled 5–4 for the residents. Courts only check **sincerity of belief** — whether you honestly hold the belief — not whether religious leaders require it.\n\n**Two Canadian approaches to religious symbols**\n- **Ontario and most of Canada:** public employees, including teachers and police, may wear religious dress.\n- **Quebec: Bill 21 (2019)** bans public employees in positions of authority — judges, police, teachers — from wearing religious symbols at work. Supporters want a visibly secular state. Critics say it falls hardest on Muslim women and Sikh men.\n\n**Think:** describe one argument for Bill 21 and one against, in your own words.'
              }
            },
            {
              title: 'Check yourself',
              visual: '✅',
              body: {
                1: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- What did **Multani** (2006) decide, and under what conditions?\n- What is the **sincerity of belief** test, and which case created it?\n\n**Exit ticket:** finish this sentence: "Accommodation is not the same as anything goes, because …"',
                2: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Give one difficulty each for a dress practice, a diet practice, and a behaviour practice in Canadian life.\n- What did Multani (2006) decide, and under what conditions?\n- What is the "sincerity of belief" test, and which case created it?\n- State one argument for and one against Quebec\'s Bill 21.\n\n**Exit ticket:** Choose one practice from today (dress, diet, or behaviour). Write one rule in a school or workplace that might conflict with it and one accommodation that would solve the conflict. Complete: "Accommodation is not the same as anything goes, because …"',
                3: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Give one difficulty each for a dress practice, a diet practice, and a behaviour practice in Canadian life.\n- What did Multani (2006) decide, and under what conditions? (The total kirpan ban violated freedom of religion; allowed sewn in cloth with a wooden sheath under clothing.)\n- What is the "sincerity of belief" test, and which case created it? (A court checks whether a belief is honestly held, not whether a religion requires it — Amselem, 2004.)\n- State one argument for and one against Quebec\'s Bill 21. (For: state neutrality/laïcité. Against: targets minorities and violates freedom of religion.)\n\n**Worked example — Lakeview Secondary reviews three rules.** "No headwear of any kind": conflicts with hijab, turban, kippah; a blanket ban is unlikely to survive and should exempt religious head coverings. "Standard school swimsuit for the swim unit": conflicts with modesty requirements; modest swimwear or alternate scheduling is reasonable. "No blades on school property": conflicts with the kirpan; after Multani a total ban is not a reasonable limit, but conditions are. A kosher-keeping student\'s cafeteria concern: label ingredients, offer sealed kosher or vegetarian options, allow outside food; undue hardship might begin if the school had to build a separate kosher kitchen at high cost — the test is cost and safety, not inconvenience.\n\n**Exit ticket:** Write one rule that might conflict with a practice and one accommodation that would solve the conflict. Look for accommodations that include conditions (like Multani) rather than simple exemptions — that distinction is the lesson\'s core. Complete: "Accommodation is not the same as anything goes, because …"',
                4: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Give one difficulty each for a dress practice, a diet practice, and a behaviour practice in Canadian life.\n- What did Multani (2006) decide, and under what conditions? (The total kirpan ban violated freedom of religion; allowed sewn in cloth with a wooden sheath under clothing.)\n- What is the "sincerity of belief" test, and which case created it? (A court checks whether a belief is honestly held, not whether a religion requires it — Amselem, 2004.)\n- State one argument for and one against Quebec\'s Bill 21. (For: state neutrality/laïcité. Against: targets minorities and violates freedom of religion.)\n\n**Worked example — Lakeview Secondary reviews three rules.** "No headwear of any kind": conflicts with hijab, turban, kippah; a blanket ban is unlikely to survive and should exempt religious head coverings. "Standard school swimsuit for the swim unit": conflicts with modesty requirements; modest swimwear or alternate scheduling is reasonable. "No blades on school property": conflicts with the kirpan; after Multani a total ban is not a reasonable limit, but conditions are. A kosher-keeping student\'s cafeteria concern: label ingredients, offer sealed kosher or vegetarian options, allow outside food; undue hardship might begin if the school had to build a separate kosher kitchen at high cost — the test is cost and safety, not inconvenience.\n\n**Exit ticket:** Write one rule that might conflict with a practice and one accommodation that would solve the conflict. Look for accommodations that include conditions (like Multani) rather than simple exemptions — that distinction is the lesson\'s core. Complete: "Accommodation is not the same as anything goes, because …"\n\n**Think like a scholar**\n- Evaluate the exit-ticket distinction between an accommodation "with conditions" and a "simple exemption." Rewrite one of the Lakeview rules both ways and explain which version a court would be more likely to accept, and why.\n- The kosher cafeteria example says undue hardship might begin at "a separate kosher kitchen at high cost." Apply the three Lesson 7 factors (cost, outside funding, health and safety): could outside funding move that line?\n- Apply today\'s cases to a rule at your own school. Is the rule written around its purpose (safety, learning) or around specific items? Rewrite it around its purpose and judge whether the rewrite would still achieve what the rule was for.'
              },
              mod: {
                k2: 'What did the judges decide about the kirpan? He could wear it safely. What do judges ask about a belief? Is it honest? Fair rules still need limits.',
                g35: 'Check yourself. What did Multani (2006) decide, and under what conditions? (A total ban was wrong; the kirpan could be worn in a wooden sheath, sewn into cloth, under clothing.) What is the sincerity of belief test, and which case created it? (Courts ask if you honestly hold a belief, not if leaders require it; Amselem, 2004.) Exit ticket: finish this sentence: "Accommodation is not the same as anything goes, because …"',
                g68: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- What did **Multani** (2006) decide, and under what conditions?\n- What is the **sincerity of belief** test, and which case created it?\n- Compare how Ontario and Quebec treat religious symbols worn by public employees.\n\n**Exit ticket:** finish this sentence: "Accommodation is not the same as anything goes, because …" Use the idea of **reasonable accommodation** — adjusting a rule so a person can follow their practice, within limits.\n\n**Think:** in Multani, what limit did the Court keep even while protecting the student\'s right?'
              }
            }
