            {
              title: {
                1: 'Spirit of Haida Gwaii',
                2: 'Real case: Bill Reid\'s Spirit of Haida Gwaii',
                3: 'Real case: Bill Reid\'s Spirit of Haida Gwaii — a canoe full of stories'
              },
              visual: '🛶',
              body: {
                1: '> Bill Reid (1920–1998) was a Haida artist. He spent years making a huge bronze canoe crowded with figures from Haida stories.\n> Raven the trickster steers. A Grizzly Bear sits at the front with Bear Mother and their two cubs.\n> Beaver, Dogfish Woman, Eagle, Frog, and Wolf are all aboard. Some bite or grip one another.\n> A small human paddles. At the centre stands a shaman with a carved staff.\n> The black bronze stands outside the Canadian Embassy in Washington. The green "Jade Canoe" is at Vancouver airport. The image was on the $20 bill from 2004 to 2012.\n> Reid said the passengers, however much they quarrel, are all in the same boat and must paddle together.',
                2: '> Bill Reid (1920–1998), a Haida artist, spent years making a huge bronze canoe crowded with figures from Haida stories: Raven the trickster steers; Mouse Woman crouches beneath him; a Grizzly Bear sits at the bow with Bear Mother and their two cubs; Beaver, Dogfish Woman, Eagle, Frog, and Wolf are all aboard, some biting or gripping one another; a small human, the "Ancient Reluctant Conscript", paddles; and at the centre stands a shaman with a staff carved with Seabear, Raven, and Killer Whale.\n> The black bronze stands outside the Canadian Embassy in Washington (1991); the green "Jade Canoe" is at Vancouver International Airport (1996); the image was on the $20 bill from 2004 to 2012. Reid said the passengers, however much they quarrel, are all in the same boat and must paddle together.',
                3: '> Bill Reid (1920–1998), a Haida artist, spent years making a huge bronze canoe crowded with figures from Haida stories: Raven the trickster steers; Mouse Woman crouches beneath him; a Grizzly Bear sits at the bow with Bear Mother and their two cubs; Beaver, Dogfish Woman, Eagle, Frog, and Wolf are all aboard, some biting or gripping one another; a small human, the "Ancient Reluctant Conscript", paddles; and at the centre stands a shaman with a staff carved with Seabear, Raven, and Killer Whale.\n> The black bronze stands outside the Canadian Embassy in Washington (1991); the green "Jade Canoe" is at Vancouver International Airport (1996); the image was on the $20 bill from 2004 to 2012. Reid said the passengers, however much they quarrel, are all in the same boat and must paddle together.\n\n**Think about it**\n- Why might Reid have chosen each figure? Pick three and connect each to what it represents in Haida stories.\n- What does a canoe full of quarrelling beings say about community? Connect to a precept from Lesson 5.\n- This sacred imagery was placed on money and at an airport. Using Unit 1\'s idea of appropriation, is that honour or misuse? Who decides?\n\n**From the notes:** figures and roles are from the Canadian Museum of History and Reid\'s own writing. Raven steering suggests the unpredictability of nature; the animals are the beings the Haida depended on; the shaman is the focal point. Reid himself approved the embassy commission, and the $20 bill was a Bank of Canada decision with the Reid estate\'s cooperation — which makes the appropriation question a genuine debate rather than an obvious case. Bill Reid was Haida through his mother.',
                4: '> Bill Reid (1920–1998), a Haida artist, spent years making a huge bronze canoe crowded with figures from Haida stories: Raven the trickster steers; Mouse Woman crouches beneath him; a Grizzly Bear sits at the bow with Bear Mother and their two cubs; Beaver, Dogfish Woman, Eagle, Frog, and Wolf are all aboard, some biting or gripping one another; a small human, the "Ancient Reluctant Conscript", paddles; and at the centre stands a shaman with a staff carved with Seabear, Raven, and Killer Whale.\n> The black bronze stands outside the Canadian Embassy in Washington (1991); the green "Jade Canoe" is at Vancouver International Airport (1996); the image was on the $20 bill from 2004 to 2012. Reid said the passengers, however much they quarrel, are all in the same boat and must paddle together.\n\n**Think about it**\n- Why might Reid have chosen each figure? Pick three and connect each to what it represents in Haida stories.\n- What does a canoe full of quarrelling beings say about community? Connect to a precept from Lesson 5.\n- This sacred imagery was placed on money and at an airport. Using Unit 1\'s idea of appropriation, is that honour or misuse? Who decides?\n\n**From the notes:** figures and roles are from the Canadian Museum of History and Reid\'s own writing. Raven steering suggests the unpredictability of nature; the animals are the beings the Haida depended on; the shaman is the focal point. Reid himself approved the embassy commission, and the $20 bill was a Bank of Canada decision with the Reid estate\'s cooperation — which makes the appropriation question a genuine debate rather than an obvious case. Bill Reid was Haida through his mother.\n\n**Close reading**\n- "Some biting or gripping one another" sits beside "all in the same boat and must paddle together": what does the sculpture claim about community by showing both at once?\n- "The Ancient Reluctant Conscript": why include a small, unwilling human among powerful story-beings, and what role does he give the viewer?\n- "Reid himself approved the embassy commission": how does that single fact change the appropriation question compared with the museum belt in Lesson 1?\n\n**Go further**\n\nThe canoe is a work of interpretation in the lesson\'s own sense. Reid did not illustrate one Haida story; he gathered figures from many and arranged them so the composition itself carries a message. The three placements, an embassy, an airport, and a banknote, then turned a Haida image into a Canadian one. Because Reid consented to the commission and his estate cooperated with the Bank of Canada, the question is genuinely open: consent and context, not location alone, decide whether public display honours or misuses sacred imagery.'
              },
              mod: {
                k2: 'Bill Reid was an artist. He made a big bronze canoe. Animals from Haida stories ride in it. They squabble, but they paddle together.',
                g35: 'Bill Reid was a Haida artist. He spent years making a huge bronze canoe called The Spirit of Haida Gwaii. Bronze is a hard metal used for statues. The canoe is crowded with figures from Haida stories: Raven steers, a grizzly bear sits at the front, and Eagle, Wolf, Frog, and others squeeze in, some biting each other. Reid said the passengers quarrel but must paddle together.',
                g68: 'Bill Reid (1920–1998), a Haida artist, spent years making a huge bronze canoe crowded with figures from Haida stories. Raven the trickster steers; a Grizzly Bear sits at the bow; Eagle, Frog, Wolf, and others are aboard, some biting or gripping one another; a small human paddles; and at the centre stands a **shaman**, a spiritual leader believed to connect with the spirit world, holding a carved staff. The black bronze stands outside the Canadian Embassy in Washington (1991), and the green Jade Canoe at Vancouver International Airport (1996). Reid said the passengers, however much they quarrel, are all in the same boat and must paddle together.\n\n**Think:** What might Reid be saying about Canada with this message?'
              }
            },
            {
              title: {
                1: 'Judging art against its story',
                2: 'Judging art against its story',
                3: 'Worked example: judging art against its story (E2.4)'
              },
              visual: '🖼️',
              body: {
                1: '**Leonardo da Vinci\'s Last Supper** shows Jesus\' final meal with his disciples.\n\n- **Faithful to the Gospels:** thirteen figures, bread and wine, the shock when Jesus announces a betrayal.\n- **The artist\'s interpretation:** a long table with everyone on one side, Italian clothing and buildings, European faces.\n- **Judgement:** true to the drama of the story, not to the real setting in first-century Jerusalem.\n\n**Images of Hindu deities** sum up stories: Ganesha\'s elephant head and Krishna\'s flute each recall a story. Different regions show the same deity differently. None is "wrong".',
                2: '**Paintings of the Last Supper**\n- The Gospels describe Jesus\' final meal with his disciples the night before his death; Leonardo da Vinci\'s mural (Milan, 1495–98) shows the moment he announces a betrayal.\n- **Faithful:** thirteen figures, bread and wine on the table, the shock of the announcement.\n- **Interpretive:** a long table with everyone on one side (for the composition), Renaissance Italian clothing and architecture, European faces, a daytime window.\n- **Judgement:** accurate to the drama of the text, not to the historical setting (a Passover-season meal in first-century Jerusalem, likely reclining).\n\n**Images of Hindu deities**\n- Each deity\'s image (murti) is a visual summary of stories: Ganesha\'s elephant head recalls the story of Shiva restoring his son\'s life; Krishna\'s flute recalls his youth among the cowherds.\n- Multiple arms show many powers at once; objects held (a lotus, a conch, a discus) each carry a story.\n- Different regions and centuries show the same deity differently; none is "wrong" — each is an interpretation within the iconography.\n\n**The three-step judgement:** what the text says, what the artwork shows, what the artist changed and why. The point is not to catch artists out but to see interpretation at work.',
                3: '**Expectation E2.4**\n\n**Paintings of the Last Supper**\n- The Gospels describe Jesus\' final meal with his disciples the night before his death; Leonardo da Vinci\'s mural (Milan, 1495–98) shows the moment he announces a betrayal.\n- **Faithful:** thirteen figures, bread and wine on the table, the shock of the announcement.\n- **Interpretive:** a long table with everyone on one side (for the composition), Renaissance Italian clothing and architecture, European faces, a daytime window.\n- **Judgement:** accurate to the drama of the text, not to the historical setting (a Passover-season meal in first-century Jerusalem, likely reclining).\n\n**Images of Hindu deities**\n- Each deity\'s image (murti) is a visual summary of stories: Ganesha\'s elephant head recalls the story of Shiva restoring his son\'s life; Krishna\'s flute recalls his youth among the cowherds.\n- Multiple arms show many powers at once; objects held (a lotus, a conch, a discus) each carry a story.\n- Different regions and centuries show the same deity differently; none is "wrong" — each is an interpretation within the iconography.\n\n**The three-step judgement:** what the text says, what the artwork shows, what the artist changed and why. The point is not to catch artists out but to see interpretation at work.\n\n**Worked example — a modern Last Supper set in a Toronto restaurant with disciples of many backgrounds.** *Faithful:* thirteen figures, bread and wine, the moment of announcement. *Interpreted:* the modern setting and diverse faces — like Leonardo\'s Renaissance Italy, which made the story the viewers\' own. *Judgement:* interpretation can strengthen meaning (the story belongs to this community too) while weakening historical accuracy; neither painting is "wrong" as art.\n\n**Worked example — a school review as a source (A3.1):** a student review calling the Reid replica "just a boat with animals" is a secondary source written to entertain or provoke; its reliability is limited if it shows no sign of consulting the Gospels, Haida sources, or the artists\' statements; the voices missing are Christian viewers, Haida people, and the artists.\n\n**Think about it:** Leonardo painted first-century Jerusalem to look like Renaissance Italy. Was that a mistake, a choice, or a way of saying "this story is ours too"? Why do festivals need the body — noise, light, food, costume — and not only the story?',
                4: '**Expectation E2.4**\n\n**Paintings of the Last Supper**\n- The Gospels describe Jesus\' final meal with his disciples the night before his death; Leonardo da Vinci\'s mural (Milan, 1495–98) shows the moment he announces a betrayal.\n- **Faithful:** thirteen figures, bread and wine on the table, the shock of the announcement.\n- **Interpretive:** a long table with everyone on one side (for the composition), Renaissance Italian clothing and architecture, European faces, a daytime window.\n- **Judgement:** accurate to the drama of the text, not to the historical setting (a Passover-season meal in first-century Jerusalem, likely reclining).\n\n**Images of Hindu deities**\n- Each deity\'s image (murti) is a visual summary of stories: Ganesha\'s elephant head recalls the story of Shiva restoring his son\'s life; Krishna\'s flute recalls his youth among the cowherds.\n- Multiple arms show many powers at once; objects held (a lotus, a conch, a discus) each carry a story.\n- Different regions and centuries show the same deity differently; none is "wrong" — each is an interpretation within the iconography.\n\n**The three-step judgement:** what the text says, what the artwork shows, what the artist changed and why. The point is not to catch artists out but to see interpretation at work.\n\n**Worked example — a modern Last Supper set in a Toronto restaurant with disciples of many backgrounds.** *Faithful:* thirteen figures, bread and wine, the moment of announcement. *Interpreted:* the modern setting and diverse faces — like Leonardo\'s Renaissance Italy, which made the story the viewers\' own. *Judgement:* interpretation can strengthen meaning (the story belongs to this community too) while weakening historical accuracy; neither painting is "wrong" as art.\n\n**Worked example — a school review as a source (A3.1):** a student review calling the Reid replica "just a boat with animals" is a secondary source written to entertain or provoke; its reliability is limited if it shows no sign of consulting the Gospels, Haida sources, or the artists\' statements; the voices missing are Christian viewers, Haida people, and the artists.\n\n**Think about it:** Leonardo painted first-century Jerusalem to look like Renaissance Italy. Was that a mistake, a choice, or a way of saying "this story is ours too"? Why do festivals need the body — noise, light, food, costume — and not only the story?\n\n**Go further**\n\nThe three-step judgement separates two kinds of accuracy that students often confuse. Leonardo is accurate to the drama of the Gospel text, since the thirteen figures, the bread and wine, and the shock of the announcement are all there, but inaccurate to its setting, since first-century Jerusalem has become Renaissance Italy. The Toronto restaurant version makes the same trade in a modern key. Hindu iconography shows that interpretation can be a system rather than a departure: a region\'s Ganesha differs from another\'s, yet each works inside shared conventions that a viewer in the tradition can read. The point in every case is to see interpretation at work and to say what it strengthens and what it costs.\n\n**Think like a scholar**\n- Apply the three steps to a film version of a sacred story you know, and decide whether its changes serve the story or the audience.\n- Evaluate: is there any change an artist could make that would stop a Last Supper from being a Last Supper?'
              },
              mod: {
                k2: 'Artists paint old stories. They keep some parts the same. They change some parts. We can ask: what did the artist change?',
                g35: 'Artists who paint a sacred story make choices. Leonardo da Vinci painted the Last Supper, Jesus\' last meal with his followers. He kept the bread, the wine, and the shock of the moment. He changed things too: everyone sits on one side of a long table. An interpretation is an artist\'s way of showing a story. Ask: what did the artist keep, and what did the artist change?',
                g68: 'The Gospels describe Jesus\' final meal with his disciples the night before his death. Leonardo da Vinci\'s mural in Milan (1495–98) shows the moment he announces a betrayal. Faithful to the text: thirteen figures, bread and wine, the shock of the announcement. Interpretive: a long table with everyone on one side, Renaissance Italian clothing and architecture. Images of Hindu deities work the same way: Ganesha\'s elephant head recalls Shiva restoring his son\'s life. **Iconography** is the set of symbols a tradition\'s art uses to show its figures and stories. Use three steps to judge art: what the text says, what the artwork shows, what the artist changed and why.\n\n**Think:** Why is the goal to see interpretation at work rather than to catch artists out?'
              }
            },
            {
              title: 'Check yourself',
              visual: '✅',
              body: {
                1: '- Tell the story behind Purim in two sentences. Name one practice that acts it out.\n- Name two figures in Spirit of Haida Gwaii.\n\nTry this: Choose one festival and write its story in one sentence, plus one practice that enacts it.',
                2: '- Tell the story behind Purim in three sentences and name three practices that enact it.\n- Which Diwali practices reflect Rama\'s return?\n- Name three figures in Spirit of Haida Gwaii and what each is doing.\n- Give one accurate and one interpretive element in Leonardo\'s Last Supper.\n\n**Exit ticket:** Choose one festival from today and write: the story in one sentence, and one practice that enacts it. Name one Unit 2 topic you want reviewed before the test.',
                3: '- Tell the story behind Purim in three sentences and name three practices that enact it.\n- Which Diwali practices reflect Rama\'s return?\n- Name three figures in Spirit of Haida Gwaii and what each is doing.\n- Give one accurate and one interpretive element in Leonardo\'s Last Supper.\n\n**Exit ticket:** Choose one festival from today and write: the story in one sentence, and one practice that enacts it. Name one Unit 2 topic you want reviewed before the test. (The usual requests are the sacred texts table from Lesson 2 and the three teachings from Lesson 3.)\n\n**Think deeper:** "Every festival is a story performed, and every sacred artwork is a story interpreted." Purim performs Esther almost line by line (reading, drowning out Haman, gifts, costumes), though the festive meal and costumes also reflect later tradition; Diwali\'s lamps perform the homecoming, but its prosperity customs and its Jain and Sikh meanings come from elsewhere — so festivals perform stories and accumulate more. Leonardo\'s Last Supper is faithful to the Gospel drama but interprets the setting as Renaissance Italy; that made the story the viewers\' own, strengthening its meaning for them while weakening historical accuracy. Why do festivals perform stories with the body rather than only telling them? The body remembers what the mind forgets; shared action builds community; re-enactment lets each generation enter the story.',
                4: '- Tell the story behind Purim in three sentences and name three practices that enact it.\n- Which Diwali practices reflect Rama\'s return?\n- Name three figures in Spirit of Haida Gwaii and what each is doing.\n- Give one accurate and one interpretive element in Leonardo\'s Last Supper.\n\n**Exit ticket:** Choose one festival from today and write: the story in one sentence, and one practice that enacts it. Name one Unit 2 topic you want reviewed before the test. (The usual requests are the sacred texts table from Lesson 2 and the three teachings from Lesson 3.)\n\n**Think deeper:** "Every festival is a story performed, and every sacred artwork is a story interpreted." Purim performs Esther almost line by line (reading, drowning out Haman, gifts, costumes), though the festive meal and costumes also reflect later tradition; Diwali\'s lamps perform the homecoming, but its prosperity customs and its Jain and Sikh meanings come from elsewhere — so festivals perform stories and accumulate more. Leonardo\'s Last Supper is faithful to the Gospel drama but interprets the setting as Renaissance Italy; that made the story the viewers\' own, strengthening its meaning for them while weakening historical accuracy. Why do festivals perform stories with the body rather than only telling them? The body remembers what the mind forgets; shared action builds community; re-enactment lets each generation enter the story.\n\n**Go further**\n\nThe think-deeper statement passes only if "performed" and "interpreted" are read as living processes. Purim performs Esther nearly line by line but its costumes and festive meal also reflect later tradition; Diwali performs the homecoming while accumulating prosperity customs and Jain and Sikh meanings; Leonardo keeps the Gospel drama while re-homing it in Italy. In each case something was added, and the addition is what made the story the community\'s own. That is why festivals need the body, since noise, light, food, and costume let each generation enter the story rather than merely hear it, and why interpretation in art strengthens meaning for viewers even while it weakens historical accuracy.\n\n**Think like a scholar**\n- Construct the strongest objection to the think-deeper statement using the Green Corn Festival, which thanks rather than re-enacts.\n- Connect: choose a celebration in your own community and identify one practice that performs its story and one that has been added since. Which do participants value more?'
              },
              mod: {
                k2: 'Tell the Purim story in your own words. Why do people light lamps at Diwali? Name one animal in Bill Reid\'s canoe.',
                g35: 'Let\'s review. Tell the story behind Purim in three sentences and name two things people do to act it out. Say which Diwali practice recalls Rama\'s return. Name two figures in The Spirit of Haida Gwaii. Give one thing Leonardo kept from the Last Supper story and one thing he changed. Exit ticket: pick one festival and write its story in one sentence, plus one practice that enacts it.',
                g68: 'Check yourself. Tell the story behind Purim in three sentences and name three practices that enact it. Explain which Diwali practices reflect Rama\'s return to Ayodhya. Name three figures in The Spirit of Haida Gwaii and what each is doing. Give one accurate and one **interpretive** element, a choice the artist made about how to show the story, in Leonardo\'s Last Supper. Exit ticket: choose one festival from today and write the story in one sentence and one practice that enacts it; then name one Unit 2 topic you want reviewed before the test.\n\n**Think:** Why do communities act out their stories instead of only reading them?'
              }
            }
