                2: 'Iftar: waiting for sunset, dates and water, a shared meal — gratitude, compassion for the hungry, community · Shabbat dinner: candles, blessings, rest from work — sacred time set apart, family peace · langar: everyone seated on the floor, same simple vegetarian food, served by volunteers — equality and service · explains that the potlatch is a specific legal and ceremonial institution of Pacific Northwest nations, banned 1885–1951, so copying its name and form is appropriation, and proposes a feast hosted by a local First Nation on its own terms · answers that fasting is not required to attend an iftar; those who fast in solidarity should follow the same health exemptions and understand the meaning rather than treating it as a challenge.',
                3: '…plus two vocabulary terms used correctly.',
