                2: 'Naming ceremony: the community (Elder, father, congregation) acts on an infant who cannot choose — the community claims the child · one-month celebration: presents the child to the community and returns the mother to it · bar/bat mitzvah: performed by the young person but witnessed and completed by the congregation (the candy, the call to the Torah), so the status change is communal recognition · concludes that the claim largely holds because rites make private change public · draws the implication: secular societies that leave coming of age to licences and birthdays give freedom but lose the witnessing that makes a status real, which may be why informal rites persist.',
