                2: '\'Daily observances do more to shape a believer than yearly festivals do.\' Evaluate this claim using Salah or the Shema, the Five Ks or the Kusti, and one festival from earlier in the unit. Consider frequency, visibility, and community, and decide which kind of practice you think shapes a person more — and why traditions keep both.',
                3: '\'Daily observances do more to shape a believer than yearly festivals do.\' Evaluate this claim using Salah or the Shema, the Five Ks or the Kusti, and one festival from earlier in the unit. Consider frequency, visibility, and community, and decide which kind of practice you think shapes a person more — and why traditions keep both.\n\nUse at least two vocabulary terms from this lesson, and give at least two specific practices for each of your three examples.',
                4: '\'Daily observances do more to shape a believer than yearly festivals do.\' Evaluate this claim using Salah or the Shema, the Five Ks or the Kusti, and one festival from earlier in the unit. Consider frequency, visibility, and community, and decide which kind of practice you think shapes a person more — and why traditions keep both.\n\nExtend your answer in one or two paragraphs: compare a practice *performed* at set times (Salah) with one *worn* at every moment (the Five Ks, the Kusti) and evaluate which does more over years — and which costs more in a secular world. Then apply the claim to your own life: name one thing you do every day without fail and one thing you do once a year, and decide honestly which has shaped you more, and why.'
