                3: '\'A tradition lives or dies by its daily observances, not its festivals or texts.\' Draw on the whole unit — a daily observance (Lesson 7), a festival (Lessons 2–4), a rite of passage (Lessons 5–6) — and on Unit 2 (texts) to evaluate this claim. Consider what each kind of practice does that the others cannot, and conclude with your own view of what keeps a tradition alive in a secular society like Canada\'s.\n\nUse at least two vocabulary terms from this lesson, and give a specific example (a named practice, festival, rite, or text) for each of the four kinds.',
                4: '\'A tradition lives or dies by its daily observances, not its festivals or texts.\' Draw on the whole unit — a daily observance (Lesson 7), a festival (Lessons 2–4), a rite of passage (Lessons 5–6) — and on Unit 2 (texts) to evaluate this claim. Consider what each kind of practice does that the others cannot, and conclude with your own view of what keeps a tradition alive in a secular society like Canada\'s.\n\nExtend your answer in one or two paragraphs: use the RCMP case to test whether daily observance is the hardest kind of practice to keep in a secular society or simply the most visible. Then apply your conclusion to a community you belong to that is not religious — a team, a family, a school — and identify its daily observance, its festival, its rite of passage, and its text. Which one, if it were dropped, would the community stop being itself?'
