            {
              title: { 1: 'Check yourself', 2: 'Check for understanding', 3: 'Check for understanding' },
              visual: '✅',
              body: {
                1: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Connect three Seder foods to the Exodus story.\n- Name two things that changed after Vatican II.\n\n**Remember:** a historical event can become a ritual, and a movement can change how people practise.',
                2: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Connect three Seder foods to the Exodus.\n- What did Reform Judaism change, and what movements formed in response?\n- Name four changes after Vatican II and two reasons lay participation grew as vowed religious declined.\n- Give one more example each of history → ritual and movement → change.\n\n**Talk it through:** If a tradition changes its practice, is it the same tradition? Who decides — leaders, members, or time?\n\n**Exit ticket:** Write one historical incident and the ritual that recalls it, from any tradition.',
                3: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Connect three Seder foods to the Exodus.\n- What did Reform Judaism change, and what movements formed in response?\n- Name four changes after Vatican II and two reasons lay participation grew as vowed religious declined.\n- Give one more example each of history → ritual and movement → change.\n\n**Worked example — comparing the two movements:** both put worship in the people\'s language, expanded lay roles (women rabbis; lay ministers), and opened to other faiths. Reform changed law and ordination more radically, while Catholicism kept the priesthood, the sacraments, and papal authority. Both provoked internal responses (Orthodox and Conservative; traditionalist Catholics). Each movement kept the ritual core and changed the surrounding form.\n\n**Talk it through**\n\n- The Seder tells each person to see themselves as freed from Egypt. Could Canada\'s Remembrance Day work that way? Should it?\n- If a tradition changes its practice, is it the same tradition? Who decides — leaders, members, or time?\n\n**Exit ticket:** Write one historical incident and the ritual that recalls it, from any tradition. For your Sacred Place Profile, name one historical event or movement that shaped your community or its building.',
                4: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Connect three Seder foods to the Exodus.\n- What did Reform Judaism change, and what movements formed in response?\n- Name four changes after Vatican II and two reasons lay participation grew as vowed religious declined.\n- Give one more example each of history → ritual and movement → change.\n\n**Worked example — comparing the two movements:** both put worship in the people\'s language, expanded lay roles (women rabbis; lay ministers), and opened to other faiths. Reform changed law and ordination more radically, while Catholicism kept the priesthood, the sacraments, and papal authority. Both provoked internal responses (Orthodox and Conservative; traditionalist Catholics). Each movement kept the ritual core and changed the surrounding form.\n\n**Talk it through**\n\n- The Seder tells each person to see themselves as freed from Egypt. Could Canada\'s Remembrance Day work that way? Should it?\n- If a tradition changes its practice, is it the same tradition? Who decides — leaders, members, or time?\n\n**Exit ticket:** Write one historical incident and the ritual that recalls it, from any tradition. For your Sacred Place Profile, name one historical event or movement that shaped your community or its building.\n\n**Go further**\n\nA thorough answer to the exit prompts shows the pattern rather than listing examples. The Seder preserves the past by making it present on every table; Reform Judaism and Vatican II adapted the present by changing language, music, and roles while keeping Torah and Shabbat, or the Mass and the sacraments. Each movement also provoked a counter-movement, which suggests that traditions decide what to change by argument over time: leaders propose, members respond, and what survives is what the community still finds meaningful.\n\n**Think like a scholar**\n\n- Evaluate the claim that rituals without adaptation become museums and adaptation without ritual loses the story. Which danger is greater for a tradition you know?\n- If a parish or congregation in your own community changed its language of worship, who should decide — leaders, members, or time? Argue with one case from this lesson.'
              },
              mod: {
                k2: 'Let us remember. A story from long ago became a holiday. The foods tell the story. Groups can change how they pray.',
                g35: 'Think about what you learned. First, a story from history became a ritual: the Exodus became the Passover Seder, and every food on the table tells part of the story. Second, a movement can change practice: Reform Judaism changed Jewish worship, and the Second Vatican Council changed the Catholic Mass. Tell a partner two Seder foods and what they stand for. Then name one thing that changed after Vatican II.',
                g68: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Connect three Seder foods to the Exodus story.\n- Name two things that changed in Catholic worship after Vatican II.\n- What did Reform Judaism change, and how did other Jews respond?\n\n**Remember:** a historical event can become a **ritual**, and a **movement**, a group working for change, can alter how people practise. Both halves of this pattern show up in every tradition.\n\n**Think:** Which is harder to change, a ritual built on an ancient story or a practice set by a council? Why?'
              }
            }
