                g35: 'Think about what you learned. Different branches of Buddhism see the Dalai Lama differently, because only Tibetan Buddhism has reborn teachers. Sunni and Shia Muslims disagree about leadership, but they share the same Qur\'an and the same pilgrimage. Tell a partner two things that unite all Muslims. Then name one role a person can play in a community, like a cantor or a Métis Senator. Remember: branches differ, but a shared founder and scripture hold a tradition together.',
                g68: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Why do Theravada Buddhists see the Dalai Lama differently from Tibetan Buddhists?\n- Name two things that unite all Muslims in the umma.\n- Give one example of a role someone plays inside a community, and what that role does.\n\n**Remember:** branches differ in leadership, texts, practices, and history, but a founder, a shared **scripture** (sacred writing), shared festivals, and the sense of being one people hold a tradition together.\n\n**Think:** Is it possible for a tradition to have no branches at all? What would that take?'
