            {
              title: {
                1: 'Real case: Amish and Mennonites',
                2: 'Real case: Old Order Amish and Conservative Mennonites',
                3: 'Real case: Old Order Amish and Conservative Mennonites — two Anabaptist neighbours in Ontario'
              },
              visual: '🐎',
              body: {
                1: '> Both groups come from the Anabaptists of the 1500s. The Anabaptists believed in adult baptism, refused to fight in wars, and stayed apart from worldly power. The Amish split from the Mennonites in 1693.\n> Ontario\'s oldest Amish settlement, near Milverton, dates to 1824.\n> **Old Order Amish** travel by horse and buggy. They do not connect to the electrical grid. They speak Pennsylvania German at home, dress plainly, worship in homes, and end school at Grade 8.\n> **Conservative Mennonites** also dress plainly and refuse to fight. But they drive cars (often plain or black), use electricity, worship in meetinghouses in English, and run their own schools.\n> Both are sincere. They draw the line between church and world in different places.',
                2: '> Both groups descend from the Anabaptists of the 1500s, who insisted on adult baptism, refusal of military service, and separation from worldly power; the Amish separated from the Mennonites in 1693 under Jakob Ammann over stricter discipline. Ontario\'s oldest Amish settlement, near Milverton, dates to 1824; Old Order Amish also live near Aylmer and elsewhere in southwestern Ontario.\n> Old Order Amish travel by horse and buggy, do not connect to the electrical grid, speak Pennsylvania German at home, dress plainly in prescribed styles, worship in homes, end formal schooling at Grade 8, and practise shunning (Meidung) of baptized members who break the Ordnung.\n> Conservative Mennonites share plain dress (head coverings for women, modest clothing), nonresistance, and a separated life, but drive cars (often plain or black), use electricity and selected technology, worship in meetinghouses in English, run their own schools, and engage in missions and evangelism.\n> Both are sincere; they draw the line between church and world in different places.',
                3: '> Both groups descend from the Anabaptists of the 1500s, who insisted on adult baptism, refusal of military service, and separation from worldly power; the Amish separated from the Mennonites in 1693 under Jakob Ammann over stricter discipline. Ontario\'s oldest Amish settlement, near Milverton, dates to 1824; Old Order Amish also live near Aylmer and elsewhere in southwestern Ontario.\n> Old Order Amish travel by horse and buggy, do not connect to the electrical grid, speak Pennsylvania German at home, dress plainly in prescribed styles, worship in homes, end formal schooling at Grade 8, and practise shunning (Meidung) of baptized members who break the Ordnung.\n> Conservative Mennonites share plain dress (head coverings for women, modest clothing), nonresistance, and a separated life, but drive cars (often plain or black), use electricity and selected technology, worship in meetinghouses in English, run their own schools, and engage in missions and evangelism.\n> Both are sincere; they draw the line between church and world in different places.\n\n**Think about it**\n\n- How do Old Order Amish differ from Conservative Mennonites? List four differences.\n- What do they share, and where does the difference come from?\n- Name two difficulties Old Order Amish may encounter in Canadian society because of their practices, and one accommodation Ontario has made.\n\n**From the speaker notes:** Shared — Anabaptist roots, adult baptism, pacifism, plain dress, community discipline. Differences — technology, transportation, language, worship place, schooling, shunning. Accommodations — buggy signage and road-shoulder provisions exist in Amish areas; the slow-moving-vehicle sign is a familiar example. Photographing Amish people is discouraged by their communities.',
                4: '> Both groups descend from the Anabaptists of the 1500s, who insisted on adult baptism, refusal of military service, and separation from worldly power; the Amish separated from the Mennonites in 1693 under Jakob Ammann over stricter discipline. Ontario\'s oldest Amish settlement, near Milverton, dates to 1824; Old Order Amish also live near Aylmer and elsewhere in southwestern Ontario.\n> Old Order Amish travel by horse and buggy, do not connect to the electrical grid, speak Pennsylvania German at home, dress plainly in prescribed styles, worship in homes, end formal schooling at Grade 8, and practise shunning (Meidung) of baptized members who break the Ordnung.\n> Conservative Mennonites share plain dress (head coverings for women, modest clothing), nonresistance, and a separated life, but drive cars (often plain or black), use electricity and selected technology, worship in meetinghouses in English, run their own schools, and engage in missions and evangelism.\n> Both are sincere; they draw the line between church and world in different places.\n\n**Think about it**\n\n- How do Old Order Amish differ from Conservative Mennonites? List four differences.\n- What do they share, and where does the difference come from?\n- Name two difficulties Old Order Amish may encounter in Canadian society because of their practices, and one accommodation Ontario has made.\n\n**From the speaker notes:** Shared — Anabaptist roots, adult baptism, pacifism, plain dress, community discipline. Differences — technology, transportation, language, worship place, schooling, shunning. Accommodations — buggy signage and road-shoulder provisions exist in Amish areas; the slow-moving-vehicle sign is a familiar example. Photographing Amish people is discouraged by their communities.\n\n**Close reading**\n\n- The passage says both groups \'draw the line between church and world in different places\'. Go through the list of Amish practices and sort each one by what it keeps out: technology, the state, or outsiders. Which category has the most entries?\n- \'Both are sincere.\' Why does the passage insert this sentence between the two descriptions, and what misreading is it guarding against?\n- Conservative Mennonites \'engage in missions and evangelism\' while the Amish do not. What does that single difference imply about how each group understands separation from the world?\n\n**Think like a scholar**\n\n- Apply Unit 1\'s sincerity test (Amselem): if a court asked \'does your religion require a horse and buggy?\', why would the Amish–Mennonite comparison make that question unanswerable?\n- Predict which group will have kept more of its young people in fifty years, and defend your prediction with evidence from the passage.'
              },
              mod: {
                k2: 'Two groups of Christians live near each other. One group drives a horse and buggy. They have no electricity at home. The other group drives plain black cars. Both groups believe in peace.',
                g35: 'Amish and Mennonites both come from the Anabaptists, Christians who believed in adult baptism and refused to fight in wars. Old Order Amish travel by horse and buggy, do not use the electrical grid, dress plainly, worship in homes, and finish school at Grade 8. Conservative Mennonites also dress plainly and refuse to fight, but they drive cars, use electricity, and worship in meetinghouses. Both groups are sincere; they just draw the line in different places.',
                g68: 'Both groups come from the **Anabaptists** of the 1500s, who believed in adult baptism, refused to fight in wars, and stayed apart from worldly power. The Amish split from the Mennonites in 1693. Ontario\'s oldest Amish settlement, near Milverton, dates to 1824.\n\n**Old Order Amish** travel by horse and buggy and do not connect to the electrical grid. They speak Pennsylvania German at home, dress plainly, worship in homes, and end school at Grade 8. Their unwritten rules are called the **Ordnung**.\n\n**Conservative Mennonites** also dress plainly and refuse to fight, but they drive cars (often plain or black), use electricity, worship in meetinghouses in English, and run their own schools.\n\nBoth are sincere. They draw the line between church and world in different places.\n\n**Think:** Why might a community choose to live without something that everyone around them uses?'
              }
            },
            {
              title: {
                1: 'Many ways to be Hindu',
                2: 'Real case: many ways to be Hindu — and spectrums within traditions',
                3: 'Real case: many ways to be Hindu — and spectrums within traditions'
              },
              visual: '🌊',
              body: {
                1: 'Hinduism has no single founder, creed, or authority. Its texts describe several paths to the divine: devotion (**bhakti**), knowledge, action, and meditation.\n\nA Hindu may keep a home shrine, visit a temple, go on pilgrimage to Varanasi, chant a mantra, follow a guru, practise yoga, fast, or be vegetarian. Or do only a few of these. For many Hindus this is the tradition\'s nature: **many rivers to one ocean**.\n\n**Other traditions have spectrums too.** In Judaism, Orthodox Jews keep full Shabbat and kosher rules, while \'cultural\' Jews keep festivals and food.',
                2: 'Hinduism has no single founder, creed, or authority, and its texts describe several paths to the divine: the path of devotion (bhakti) to a chosen deity; the path of knowledge (jnana) through study and meditation; the path of action (karma yoga) through duty done without attachment; and the path of discipline (raja yoga) through meditation. A Hindu may keep a home shrine with daily puja, attend a temple weekly or only at festivals, go on pilgrimage to Varanasi, chant a mantra on beads, follow a guru, practise yoga, fast on Ekadashi, keep strict vegetarianism — or do a few of these, or almost none, while still identifying as Hindu. The Bhagavad Gita presents Krishna accepting worship offered in any form with sincerity. For many Hindus, this plurality is the tradition\'s nature: many rivers to one ocean.\n\n**Spectrums within traditions**\n\n| Tradition | Strict observance | Moderate and liberal observance |\n| Judaism | Haredi and Modern Orthodox — full Shabbat and kosher observance | Conservative and Reform; \'cultural\' Jews who keep festivals and food |\n| Islam | five daily prayers, full Ramadan fast, hijab or beard | Friday prayer and Ramadan; dress varies |\n| Sikhism | initiated (amritdhari) Khalsa with the Five Ks | keshdhari (uncut hair, not initiated) and sahajdhari (cut hair) Sikhs |',
                3: 'Hinduism has no single founder, creed, or authority, and its texts describe several paths to the divine: the path of devotion (bhakti) to a chosen deity; the path of knowledge (jnana) through study and meditation; the path of action (karma yoga) through duty done without attachment; and the path of discipline (raja yoga) through meditation. A Hindu may keep a home shrine with daily puja, attend a temple weekly or only at festivals, go on pilgrimage to Varanasi, chant a mantra on beads, follow a guru, practise yoga, fast on Ekadashi, keep strict vegetarianism — or do a few of these, or almost none, while still identifying as Hindu. The Bhagavad Gita presents Krishna accepting worship offered in any form with sincerity. Regional traditions, family deities, and caste and community customs add more variety. For many Hindus, this plurality is not a weakness but the tradition\'s nature: many rivers to one ocean.\n\n**Worked example — why Hindus may express devotion in a multiplicity of ways:** no central authority; multiple scriptural paths; the Gita\'s acceptance of sincere worship in any form; regional and family variety; the \'many paths\' view itself.\n\n**Spectrums within traditions**\n\n| Tradition | Strict observance | Moderate and liberal observance |\n| Judaism | Haredi and Modern Orthodox — full Shabbat and kosher observance, daily prayer, gender-separate seating | Conservative and Reform — selective observance, egalitarian worship; \'cultural\' Jews who keep festivals and food |\n| Islam | five daily prayers, full Ramadan fast, hijab or beard, no alcohol | Friday prayer and Ramadan, fewer daily prayers; dress varies |\n| Christianity | daily Mass or prayer, Friday abstinence, religious orders, Old Order Amish | weekly or holiday attendance; \'cultural Catholics\' who keep baptism, weddings, Christmas |\n| Sikhism | initiated (amritdhari) Khalsa with the Five Ks and Nitnem | keshdhari (uncut hair, not initiated) and sahajdhari (cut hair) Sikhs |\n| Hinduism | daily puja, strict vegetarianism, fasts, pilgrimage, a guru | festivals and family rites without daily practice |\n\n**Think about it**\n\n- Compare with the Amish: one tradition holds a tight line, the other many lines. Which is harder to keep alive in a secular society, and why? (Notes: a tight line is visible and demanding, while plurality absorbs change but can blur identity — both are strategies.)\n- Where does \'liberal compromise\' end and leaving the tradition begin, in a tradition with no single rule?\n\nDo not treat the right column as \'less Muslim\' or \'less Jewish\': the tradition makes room.',
                4: 'Hinduism has no single founder, creed, or authority, and its texts describe several paths to the divine: the path of devotion (bhakti) to a chosen deity; the path of knowledge (jnana) through study and meditation; the path of action (karma yoga) through duty done without attachment; and the path of discipline (raja yoga) through meditation. A Hindu may keep a home shrine with daily puja, attend a temple weekly or only at festivals, go on pilgrimage to Varanasi, chant a mantra on beads, follow a guru, practise yoga, fast on Ekadashi, keep strict vegetarianism — or do a few of these, or almost none, while still identifying as Hindu. The Bhagavad Gita presents Krishna accepting worship offered in any form with sincerity. Regional traditions, family deities, and caste and community customs add more variety. For many Hindus, this plurality is not a weakness but the tradition\'s nature: many rivers to one ocean.\n\n**Worked example — why Hindus may express devotion in a multiplicity of ways:** no central authority; multiple scriptural paths; the Gita\'s acceptance of sincere worship in any form; regional and family variety; the \'many paths\' view itself.\n\n**Spectrums within traditions**\n\n| Tradition | Strict observance | Moderate and liberal observance |\n| Judaism | Haredi and Modern Orthodox — full Shabbat and kosher observance, daily prayer, gender-separate seating | Conservative and Reform — selective observance, egalitarian worship; \'cultural\' Jews who keep festivals and food |\n| Islam | five daily prayers, full Ramadan fast, hijab or beard, no alcohol | Friday prayer and Ramadan, fewer daily prayers; dress varies |\n| Christianity | daily Mass or prayer, Friday abstinence, religious orders, Old Order Amish | weekly or holiday attendance; \'cultural Catholics\' who keep baptism, weddings, Christmas |\n| Sikhism | initiated (amritdhari) Khalsa with the Five Ks and Nitnem | keshdhari (uncut hair, not initiated) and sahajdhari (cut hair) Sikhs |\n| Hinduism | daily puja, strict vegetarianism, fasts, pilgrimage, a guru | festivals and family rites without daily practice |\n\n**Think about it**\n\n- Compare with the Amish: one tradition holds a tight line, the other many lines. Which is harder to keep alive in a secular society, and why? (Notes: a tight line is visible and demanding, while plurality absorbs change but can blur identity — both are strategies.)\n- Where does \'liberal compromise\' end and leaving the tradition begin, in a tradition with no single rule?\n\nDo not treat the right column as \'less Muslim\' or \'less Jewish\': the tradition makes room.\n\n**Go further**\n\nThe Hindu case and the spectrum table answer the same question from two directions. Hinduism makes room by its very structure — no single founder, creed, or authority; four named paths; the Gita\'s acceptance of sincere worship in any form — so a believer who does almost none of the listed practices still belongs. The traditions in the table make room by other means: separate denominations (Judaism), informal variation inside one community (Islam), or formal named categories (Sikhism\'s amritdhari, keshdhari, sahajdhari). The lesson\'s point is that *every* tradition has a spectrum; what differs is whether the spectrum is named, formalized, or left unspoken.\n\n**Think like a scholar**\n\n- \'Many rivers to one ocean\': would interfaith life in Ontario be easier if more traditions held this view, or does it only work from inside Hinduism?\n- Compare the right-hand column across traditions. Is a \'cultural\' member who keeps festivals and food but not belief still a member? Who decides — the person, the community, or the tradition\'s rules?'
              },
              mod: {
                k2: 'Hindus show love for God in many ways. Some pray at a small shrine at home. Some visit a temple. Some sing or do yoga. All these ways are good.',
                g35: 'Hinduism has no single founder or leader, and it offers several paths to the divine: devotion, knowledge, action, and meditation. Bhakti means loving devotion, like the way you might show love for a grandparent with cards, visits, and hugs. A Hindu may keep a shrine at home, visit a temple, chant a mantra, practise yoga, fast, or be vegetarian. Or do only a few of these. Many Hindus say it is like many rivers flowing to one ocean.',
                g68: 'Hinduism has no single founder, creed, or authority. Its texts describe several paths to the divine: devotion (**bhakti**, loving devotion to a deity), knowledge, action, and meditation.\n\nA Hindu may keep a home shrine, visit a temple, go on pilgrimage to Varanasi, chant a mantra, follow a guru, practise yoga, fast, or be vegetarian. Or do only a few of these. For many Hindus this is the tradition\'s nature: **many rivers to one ocean**.\n\n**Other traditions have spectrums too.** In Judaism, Orthodox Jews keep full Shabbat and kosher rules, while \'cultural\' Jews keep festivals and food.\n\n**Think:** What might be a strength of a tradition with many paths? What might be a challenge?'
              }
            },
            {
              title: { 1: 'Check yourself', 2: 'Check for understanding', 3: 'Check for understanding' },
              visual: '✅',
              body: {
                1: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Name two ways Old Order Amish differ from Conservative Mennonites.\n- Name two ways a Hindu might show devotion.\n\n**Remember:** both strict and relaxed members can be sincere. The tradition makes room for both.',
                2: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- How do Old Order Amish differ from Conservative Mennonites? What do they share?\n- Why may Hindus express devotion in a multiplicity of ways?\n- Give a strict and a liberal example of observance in two traditions.\n- Name two ways traditions make room for a range of commitment.\n\n**Talk it through:** Is a \'cultural\' member of a tradition — festivals and food, no belief — still a member? Who decides: the person, the community, or the tradition\'s rules?\n\n**Exit ticket:** Write one tradition, one strict practice, and one liberal practice, and name how the tradition accommodates both.',
                3: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- How do Old Order Amish differ from Conservative Mennonites? What do they share?\n- Why may Hindus express devotion in a multiplicity of ways?\n- Give a strict and a liberal example of observance in two traditions.\n- Name two ways traditions make room for a range of commitment.\n\n**Worked example (model answer):** Differences — horse and buggy vs cars; no grid electricity vs electricity and selected technology; Pennsylvania German vs English worship; worship in homes vs meetinghouses; schooling to Grade 8 vs own schools; shunning vs less formal discipline; no evangelism vs missions. Shared — Anabaptist roots, adult baptism, pacifism/nonresistance, plain dress, separation from the world, community discipline.\n\n**Talk it through**\n\n- The Amish keep their community by limiting technology; most traditions in Canada have not. Fifty years from now, which strategy will have kept more of its young people? Why?\n- Is a \'cultural\' member of a tradition — festivals and food, no belief — still a member? Who decides: the person, the community, or the tradition\'s rules?\n- Hinduism says many rivers lead to one ocean. Would that view make interfaith life in Canada easier if more traditions held it, or does it only work from inside Hinduism?\n\n**Exit ticket:** Write one tradition, one strict practice, and one liberal practice, and name how the tradition accommodates both. For your Sacred Place Profile: where on its tradition\'s spectrum does your community sit, and how does the place show it (dress expectations, language of worship, who leads)?',
                4: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- How do Old Order Amish differ from Conservative Mennonites? What do they share?\n- Why may Hindus express devotion in a multiplicity of ways?\n- Give a strict and a liberal example of observance in two traditions.\n- Name two ways traditions make room for a range of commitment.\n\n**Worked example (model answer):** Differences — horse and buggy vs cars; no grid electricity vs electricity and selected technology; Pennsylvania German vs English worship; worship in homes vs meetinghouses; schooling to Grade 8 vs own schools; shunning vs less formal discipline; no evangelism vs missions. Shared — Anabaptist roots, adult baptism, pacifism/nonresistance, plain dress, separation from the world, community discipline.\n\n**Talk it through**\n\n- The Amish keep their community by limiting technology; most traditions in Canada have not. Fifty years from now, which strategy will have kept more of its young people? Why?\n- Is a \'cultural\' member of a tradition — festivals and food, no belief — still a member? Who decides: the person, the community, or the tradition\'s rules?\n- Hinduism says many rivers lead to one ocean. Would that view make interfaith life in Canada easier if more traditions held it, or does it only work from inside Hinduism?\n\n**Exit ticket:** Write one tradition, one strict practice, and one liberal practice, and name how the tradition accommodates both. For your Sacred Place Profile: where on its tradition\'s spectrum does your community sit, and how does the place show it (dress expectations, language of worship, who leads)?\n\n**Go further**\n\nA thorough answer to the exit prompt names *how* the tradition accommodates, not only that it does: separate branches where the line became a denomination; named categories; levels of vow (laity, third orders, vowed religious; five precepts for Buddhist laity and hundreds for monks); minimum and maximum (obligatory and recommended acts); and life stages that expect more of elders. The bridge to Unit 1 follows directly: because the same practice is required for one believer and optional for another, the law tests sincerity rather than doctrine.\n\n**Think like a scholar**\n\n- Evaluate the claim that a tight line keeps a tradition\'s identity while many ways keep its members. Use one case from this lesson for each half, and say what each strategy risks in a secular society like Ontario\'s.\n- Choose a community you know. Where would you place it on its tradition\'s spectrum, and how does its building or meeting place show it (dress, language, who leads)?'
              },
              mod: {
                k2: 'Let us remember. Some people follow their religion very closely. Some follow it more loosely. Both can truly mean it.',
                g35: 'Think about what you learned. Old Order Amish and Conservative Mennonites share the same roots, but one group drives a horse and buggy while the other drives a car. Hindus can show devotion at a home shrine, in a temple, through yoga, or in many other ways. Tell a partner one difference between the Amish and the Mennonites. Then name two ways a Hindu might show devotion. Remember: strict and relaxed members can both be sincere.',
                g68: 'Answer in your notes, then compare with a partner.\n\n- Name two ways Old Order Amish differ from Conservative Mennonites.\n- Name two ways a Hindu might show devotion.\n- Give one example of a **spectrum** (a range from strict to relaxed) in a tradition other than Hinduism.\n\n**Remember:** both strict and relaxed members can be sincere. A tradition makes room for both through branches, categories, levels of vow, and different expectations at different stages of life.\n\n**Think:** Why might the same tradition look very different in two neighbouring towns?'
              }
            }
