                2: '\'A tradition that draws a tight line keeps its identity; a tradition that allows many ways keeps its members.\' Evaluate this claim using the Old Order Amish, Conservative Mennonites, and Hinduism, and one spectrum from another tradition. For each, consider what the strategy protects and what it risks in a secular society like Ontario\'s, and conclude with your own view of how traditions should handle the range of commitment.',
                3: '\'A tradition that draws a tight line keeps its identity; a tradition that allows many ways keeps its members.\' Evaluate this claim using the Old Order Amish, Conservative Mennonites, and Hinduism, and one spectrum from another tradition. For each, consider what the strategy protects and what it risks in a secular society like Ontario\'s, and conclude with your own view of how traditions should handle the range of commitment.\n\nFor each of the four cases give at least two specific practices from the lesson as evidence, and name how the tradition makes room (separate branches, named categories, levels of vow, minimum and maximum, life stages).',
                4: '\'A tradition that draws a tight line keeps its identity; a tradition that allows many ways keeps its members.\' Evaluate this claim using the Old Order Amish, Conservative Mennonites, and Hinduism, and one spectrum from another tradition. For each, consider what the strategy protects and what it risks in a secular society like Ontario\'s, and conclude with your own view of how traditions should handle the range of commitment.\n\nWrite one to two paragraphs. Then test your conclusion against the Conservative Mennonites, who moved the line rather than keeping or removing it: does their case show a third strategy, and is it sustainable? Finish by predicting which of the four traditions will have kept more of its young people in fifty years, and connect your prediction to a community you know.'
